"Judicial Council of California - File #: 26-133 (https://jcc.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?GUID=FC917095-4A7A-4B1D-B8D7-ADC56684A26B&ID=8212126)\nciteturn2620search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Title: Rules and Forms | Unlawful Detainer: Form Revisions to Implement Assembly Bills 246, 747, and 863 (Action Required) ... 26-133 RnF Unlawful Detainer-Form Revisions to Implement ABs 246, 747, and 863 RPT\n\n# Legislation Details\n\nFile #: 26-133    Version: 1\nType: Rule/Form Proposal Status: Consent Agenda\nFile created: 7/27/2026 In control: Judicial Council\nOn agenda: 10/16/2026 Final action:\nTitle: Rules and Forms | Unlawful Detainer: Form Revisions to Implement Assembly Bills 246, 747, and 863 (Action Required)\nAttachments: 1. 26-133 RnF Unlawful Detainer-Form Revisions to Implement ABs 246, 747, and 863 RPT\nDate [Input][Input][Input] Action By[Input][Input] Action[Input][Input] Result[Input][Input] Action Details[Input][Input] Meeting Details[Input][Input] Video[Input][Input]\nNo records to display.\n[Input]\nTitle Title Rules and Forms | Unlawful Detainer: Form Revisions to Implement Assembly Bills 246, 747, and 863 (Action Required) body Summary Summary Assembly Bills 246 (Stats. 2025, ch. 337), 747 (Stats. 2025, ch. 563) and 863 (Stats. 2025, ch. 344) make changes to laws regarding summonses, complaints, and defenses in unlawful detainer proceedings. The Civil and Small Claims Advisory Committee recommends revising four forms to implement these changes in law, correct misstatements of law, remove inapplicable items, and improve readability and clarity. The committee also recommends significant plain-language revisions to Unlawful Detainer--Complaint (form UD-100) and the conversion of Unlawful Detainer--Answer (form UD-105) to a plain-language form. body Recommendation Recommendation [Enter Recommendation Here] body [Input]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeeting Materials (https://courts.ca.gov/system/files/file/rules-20250904-materials.pdf)\nciteturn2620search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Civil Practice and Procedure: Form Revisions to Reflect the Repeal of COVID-19 Legislation (Revise forms SC-100, SC-103, SC-104B, SUM-130, UD-100, UD-105; revoke forms PLD-C-500, PLD-C-505, PLD-C-520, SC-500, SC-500-INFO, SC-500A, UD-101, UD-104, UD-104(A), UD-125) ... • In September 2026 the landlord files an unlawful detainer based on that notice.\nSPR25-07  \nCivil Practice and Procedure: Form Revisions to Reflect the Repeal of COVID-19 Legislation (Revise forms SC-100, SC-103, SC-104B, SUM-130, UD-100, UD-105; revoke forms PLD-C-500, PLD-C-505, PLD-C-520, SC-500, SC-500-INFO, SC-500A, UD-101, UD-104, UD-104(A), UD-125)\n\nAll comments are verbatim unless indicated by an asterisk (*).\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Commenter</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Comment</th>\n<th>Committee Response</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>\n• In August 2026 the landlord issues a notice to quit based on nonpayment.  \n• In September 2026 the landlord files an unlawful detainer based on that notice.  \n• The tenant can still raise Item 3.p. as a defense because at the time the misapplication of rent occurred (September 2025), the law (CCP § 1179.04.5) was still in effect (until October 2025). The filing of the lawsuit a year later does not remove this defense.\n\nThus, removing this defense prematurely will deny tenants the ability to easily raise this defense while allowing landlords to file cases where it may be warranted without any additional barriers. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms/all-by-category)\nciteturn2620search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago;   * CARE-103 Order to Provide Information to Respondent's Attorney in Related Proceedings Effective: September 1, 2024 汉语 فارسی 한국어 español 